# Shopify Horizon Development Rules

## Core objective
Build custom UI/UX on top of Horizon while keeping the theme update-safe, modular, editor-friendly, and easy to maintain.

**Accessibility:** Always follow WCAG-oriented, keyboard-first practices. See `.cursor/rules/accessibility.mdc` and `.cursor/rules/global-accessibility-standards.mdc` (and component-specific `*-accessibility.mdc` rules when they apply).

## Architecture rules
- Treat Horizon as the upstream base theme, not as a blank custom theme.
- Prefer additive customization over invasive modification.
- Build UI as reusable:
  - sections
  - blocks
  - theme blocks
  - snippets
- Do not hardcode page-specific layouts when the same pattern can be reused across templates.
- Do not duplicate markup for similar UI patterns; extract shared snippets or theme blocks.
- Keep business logic minimal in sections. Reusable rendering logic should live in snippets/helpers where possible.
- Preserve compatibility with Shopify theme editor behavior and Horizon conventions.

## Horizon-specific rules
- Assume Horizon already provides core primitives for:
  - header
  - sticky behavior
  - mega menu
  - search
  - predictive search
  - filtering/sorting
- Extend existing Horizon primitives before replacing them.
- Reuse existing Horizon patterns for:
  - product grid
  - product cards
  - search results
  - predictive search
  - section/block rendering
- Before creating a new section/snippet/block, search the Horizon codebase for an existing equivalent and adapt it.
- Prefer **duplicate Horizon file → rename with a custom prefix → modify** over greenfield files. Naming: see `.cursor/rules/horizon-modular-naming.mdc` (always-on).
- Do not rewrite Horizon core files unless there is no safe extension path.

## Update-safe customization rules
- Minimize long-lived diffs against upstream Horizon.
- Prefer creating new custom sections/blocks/snippets over editing core Horizon files.
- If a core Horizon file must be changed:
  - keep the change minimal
  - document why it was necessary
  - avoid unrelated refactors
  - preserve existing schema/settings behavior where possible
- Never scatter one feature across many unrelated files unless required.
- Keep custom code namespaced and easy to locate.
- Favor schema-driven controls over hardcoded values.

## Theme editor compatibility rules
- All sections and blocks must work correctly inside the Shopify theme editor.
- JavaScript must support section re-rendering in the editor.
- Re-initialize interactive behavior on Shopify editor events such as section load/select when needed.
- Do not rely only on initial page load for JS behavior.
- Include `{{ block.shopify_attributes }}` on rendered blocks where applicable.
- Avoid DOM patterns that break when Shopify swaps section HTML dynamically.
- Do not add code that interferes with native Shopify editor/admin behavior. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2} :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}

## Section and block design rules
- Use theme blocks for repeatable UI atoms/molecules used across multiple sections.
- Use section-local blocks only when the block is meaningful only inside one section.
- Use static blocks when structure must not be removable/reorderable by merchants.
- Use dynamic blocks when merchants should be able to reorder content.
- Expose layout/content options through schema:
  - spacing
  - alignment
  - colors when appropriate
  - visibility toggles
  - collection/product/content source selection
- Merchant-editable content must come from settings, metafields, metaobjects, or dynamic sources when appropriate.
- Avoid hardcoded content in Liquid unless it is truly structural/developer-only.

## Header and footer rules
- Header and footer must follow section-group architecture.
- Do not hardcode header/footer structure directly into layout when it should be editor-managed.
- For header customization:
  - keep primary header group intact
  - extend it with modular sections/blocks
  - preserve existing accessibility and sticky behavior
- Expose brand/header variations via schema instead of branching templates unnecessarily. :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}

## Search and filtering rules
- Search UI must build on Shopify search primitives, not custom unsupported search logic.
- Reuse product-grid/product-card rendering between collection pages and search result pages wherever possible.
- Predictive search should use Shopify’s predictive search pattern, not a custom fake implementation.
- Filtering must rely on Shopify storefront filtering and Search & Discovery-compatible data.
- Do not implement tag-hack filtering if proper metafield/storefront filtering is available.
- Filter UI may be custom visually, but must sit on top of Shopify’s real filtering objects/data.
- Gracefully handle cases where filtering data is unavailable or limited.

## Data modeling rules
- Use:
  - metafields for per-resource structured fields
  - metaobjects for reusable structured content across many pages/resources
  - dynamic sources for merchant-friendly binding in the editor
- For rich editorial or reusable content, prefer metaobjects over hardcoded repeated section settings.
- Use multiple templates per resource type when different layouts are genuinely needed.
- Do not hardcode data that belongs in admin-managed content structures.

## JavaScript rules
- Use JavaScript only where enhancement is needed.
- Follow progressive enhancement:
  - core content and navigation should render server-side in Liquid
  - JS should enhance, not gate core functionality
- Keep JS modular and section-aware.
- Avoid heavy global scripts.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies when native JS is sufficient.
- Preserve performance and avoid layout-shift-heavy patterns.

## CSS rules
- Prefer existing Horizon styling patterns and tokens before inventing new ones.
- Scope styles to the component/section namespace.
- Do not write overly broad selectors that can leak across templates.
- Preserve responsiveness across desktop, tablet, and mobile.
- Match existing design system spacing and behavior where possible.
- Do not change unrelated existing styles while implementing a feature.

## Liquid rules
- Write clear, explicit Liquid.
- Use descriptive variable names.
- Keep conditions readable and shallow where possible.
- Avoid duplicated logic.
- Prefer snippets for repeated rendering fragments.
- Preserve Shopify schema validity.
- Do not break editor configurability by hardcoding values that should remain configurable.
- Use `content_for` / block rendering patterns consistent with Horizon when applicable. :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}

## Accessibility rules
- Preserve semantic HTML.
- Buttons must be buttons; links must be links.
- Maintain keyboard accessibility for menus, drawers, modals, tabs, and carousels.
- Preserve or improve ARIA labels, focus management, and visible states.
- Do not introduce interactions that work only on hover.
- Mobile interactions must be tap-friendly.

## Performance rules
- Default to lightweight implementations.
- Avoid unnecessary JS for static presentation.
- Reuse existing Horizon snippets/components before adding new assets.
- Lazy-load non-critical media/features where appropriate.
- Do not introduce oversized libraries for small UI behavior.
- Optimize for progressive rendering and maintainable storefront performance. Horizon emphasizes progressive enhancement and performance-first delivery. :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6} :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}

## App / extension boundary rules
- Keep storefront presentation in the theme unless backend/app capability is truly required.
- Use theme app extensions or app embeds only when the feature belongs outside direct theme code ownership.
- Do not propose checkout theme customizations for Information/Shipping/Payment steps.
- Checkout customization must be treated as Checkout Extensibility work, and some surfaces require Shopify Plus. :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8} :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}

## Git and workflow rules
- Treat the project as:
  - Horizon upstream
  - local customization layer
- Keep feature branches short-lived.
- Avoid mixing refactors with feature work.
- Keep commits scoped and descriptive.
- When editing upstream-like files, make changes easy to diff and rebase later.
- Document any unavoidable divergence from Horizon core behavior.

## What Cursor should do before coding
Before generating code, Cursor must:
1. search the existing theme for similar patterns
2. identify whether the feature should be:
   - a new section
   - a new block
   - a theme block
   - a snippet extension
   - a minimal patch to an existing Horizon component
3. prefer the most update-safe option
4. preserve editor configurability
5. avoid replacing core Horizon behavior unless necessary

## What Cursor should not do
- Do not rebuild Horizon from scratch.
- Do not hardcode content that merchants need to edit.
- Do not replace Shopify-native filtering/search with custom ad hoc logic.
- Do not create giant all-in-one sections when smaller reusable parts are better.
- Do not write JS that only works after full page load.
- Do not introduce breaking changes to theme editor behavior.
- Do not make unrelated style or structural changes while solving a focused task.
- Do not modify checkout behavior through theme code.
- Do not remove existing schema/settings unless explicitly requested.

## Output expectations for every task
For each implementation, provide:
- files to create/update
- why this approach is update-safe
- what existing Horizon pattern is being reused
- what settings/schema are added
- how editor compatibility is preserved
- mobile behavior notes
- any known Shopify platform constraints